{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And all the goods of his master in his hand.</b> The verse should have said ומטוב אדוניו — 'from the goods of his master' — as it says מגמלי אדוניו, for ten camels could not contain all the goods of his master. But by way of the plain sense (peshat), Scripture testifies to the excellence of the servant and to his faithfulness, and declares that all the wealth and property of Abraham was in his authority, for he had been established with him as a trustworthy man, and he placed everything in his hand — that is, in his authority, as in (Numbers 21:26): ״ויקח את כל ארצו מידו״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'And all the goods of his master in his hand' — this is a gift-deed (דייתיקי), that is, a deed of gift that he wrote for Isaac, of all that he has, so that they would rush to send him their daughter. And the word 'in his hand' proves it, and likewise the word וילך — meaning that he went with all the goods of his master that were in his hand, which is the deed of gift. And therefore it repeats a second time ויקם וילך, which is 'and he set his feet to the road.' Now according to this opinion, what is written ״ויתן אברהם את כל אשר לו ליצחק״ — the intent is that he secured him in the assets at the time of his passing, so that the sons of Keturah would not contest him, as in what is written (Genesis 25:6): ״וישלחם מעל יצחק בנו בעודנו חי״ וגו׳."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:38.798846702Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}